592186281264 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281264 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281264 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281264 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f40528cb-fde2-5a1d-88ab-741000eebbb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281581 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b80ece25-e7d4-5756-bce7-b1813e029457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281581 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=2x{y}^{3}, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281591 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281591 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281591 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281591 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+xy,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281591 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2ef07bc-cc92-59f4-ab07-cedcba745cfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281591 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy)=2x+y,\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y & x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-2=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2+(-3)+(-2)=-3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281598 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{2}+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}e \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}e \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47bfa96d-bfdf-5b9f-a011-3a66ebe7f2b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281598 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{{x}_{1}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{2})=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}^{2}+1)=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}^{2}+1)=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{-1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}e \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}e \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+{e}^{-1}+(-2)={e}^{-1}-2.