{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} & \\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{2}{3}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186442211 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186442219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442211 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186442220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442211 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 1759218644222